 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T08:45+08:00",
 "answer": "Yes, the haze in Johor Bahru (and the broader Johor region) is generally **worse** than in Singapore. \n\nBoth regions are experiencing unhealthy air quality due to regional transboundary haze and local hotspots (such as peat fires). However:\n* **Johor Bahru / Johor:** Air Pollutant Index (API) readings in parts of Johor have frequently climbed higher into the upper \"Unhealthy\" bracket (with some areas in the state pushing close to or above 150–190+). The severity in Johor has even prompted local authorities to order school closures in severely affected districts like Batu Pahat. \n* **Singapore:** Singapore's 24-hour Pollutant Standards Index (PSI) has also hovered in the unhealthy range (roughly 144 to 191), but overall, parts of southern Malaysia—being closer to local agricultural fires and specific hotspots like Pasir Gudang and Pengerang—frequently register higher peak pollution numbers than Singapore on a day-to-day basis. \n\nBecause wind conditions shift dynamically, the exact comparison can fluctuate by the hour, but Johor generally bears a heavier brunt of the immediate smoke plumes.",
